00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.760
So a little bit
more dryly, here's

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.320
what the problem actually is.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.410
So yeah, professor,
what's a Skolem function?

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.230
So you've got a
first order sentence.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.600
So this is a Boolean formula.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.250
My variables are all quantified.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.600
I have two types of
quantifiers for all and exists,

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.090
and I'd really rather
just have one of them.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.190
So in particular,
let's suppose I

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.950
want to get rid of these
existential quantifiers

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.930
so that I'm just dealing
with some sort of--

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.910
I just want something
that's a true statement

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.460
irrespective of then the
binding of the remaining

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.770
free variables.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:24.340
And so a Skolemization
is where you're

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.090
going to replace those
existentially bound variables

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.630
with functions of variables
that were previously bound,

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:37.510
which are basically going
to capture what would be

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.420
a successful binding of those
variables given the things that

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:45.110
were previously bound
outside in that scope.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:47.990
And those-- so it eliminates
the existentials, and those

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.350
are called Skolem functions.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.380
So to be precise--

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.380
and it actually matters here--

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.598
so if I have this first order
Boolean sentence-- and let's

00:04:01.598 --> 00:04:03.890
suppose the body here could
be another Boolean sentence

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:05.480
on more variables.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.240
If I have a function
that takes that, say,

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:16.130
one existentially bound
variable and maps it--

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.790
again, given a setting of the
previously bound variables--

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:26.880
to something that preserves the
truth value of the sentence,

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.960
then that's a Skolem function.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:35.150
And again, I'm saying
preserves the truth value

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.690
because if the sentence was
not true to begin with--

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:43.493
if there was some binding of
w for which there was no x,

00:04:43.493 --> 00:04:45.660
this thing would have been
false, and you might say,

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.160
well, there's no
Skolem function.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.760
In this case, we're
going to let that just

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.420
be an arbitrary binding
of the variable.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.860
So it's always going to exist.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.290
In some cases, it
might be that it

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:05.310
doesn't matter what the function
value is for that binding of w.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.650
We're happy with anything.

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.630
And so very broad strokes--

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.400
the problem that
we're considering here

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:19.760
is to count the number of
Skolem functions for a formula

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.040
now that actually--
this is just going

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.190
to be a propositional
formula, so I just

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.160
have a single alternation.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.670
And to make things
relatively simple,

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:34.160
all the domains are just
going to be Boolean.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.085
So n bits, m bits.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.860
Yeah, so it's a--

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:48.840
so from a very general
problem to a much simpler one.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.073
What's that?

00:05:50.073 --> 00:05:51.990
(INCREDULOUS) You think
this is just a problem

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.520
that we just made up?

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.490
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:57.900
[END PLAYBACK]

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.900
So what is it good for?

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.450
So here's one possible
application of this.

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:08.550
So in system security,
a natural technique

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:12.930
is randomization
of your components.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:17.730
And traditionally, the way
this is done is by, say,

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.590
taking the code and
applying some sort

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.810
of a transformation on it
that preserves the semantics.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.570
A very common thing is like
address space randomization

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:30.780
to try to make it hard to
clobber important values

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.530
in your memory.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.170
A slightly different take
on it that we have here is,

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:41.010
let's suppose that we have a
specification for the function.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.130
Now, if I look at
this sentence--

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.350
so for all inputs,
there is an output

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.000
meeting the specification--

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.550
the Skolem count
for that sentence

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:57.460
is the number of
possible functions

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.340
that satisfy the specification.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.250
And so if I had a
large count that's

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:06.430
telling me that somehow many
possible implementations

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:09.310
of this specification
are possible-- and so

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:13.810
then there's some scope for
being able to get something out

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:19.310
of randomizing over which
implementation you use.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:22.690
Furthermore, using a reduction
of approximate sampling

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.610
to approximate
counting, it would

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:28.090
be possible to simulate the
evaluation of a random function

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:32.060
meeting that specification at
some points of your choice.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.300
Now, I'm not going to claim
that this is really fast.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.690
I wouldn't actually want
to use a system that

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:41.043
was built on top of this,
but it's at least telling you

00:07:41.043 --> 00:07:42.460
that you might be
able to use this

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.630
for some kind of prototyping.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.150
See how it works.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.030
A second application would
be modeling uncertainty

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.440
of player strategies.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.940
So again, let's suppose that
I have a formula indicating

00:07:55.940 --> 00:08:02.330
if x is a good response to some
game state or initial move w.

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:08.480
Then, a random Skolem function
is a maximum entropy good

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:09.440
response strategy.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:16.310
And so I can try to
use that as a model of,

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:19.760
what are the possible strategies
that an opponent could

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:20.900
be using in this game?

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.490
And then the Skolem
count, in turn,

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.710
is giving you the entropy
of those strategies.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:29.990
So these applications
are a little bit made up.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:31.490
And the third
application is I would

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:35.270
like to hear from you if you can
think of any more applications

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:40.440
for this problem, because
it is a new problem.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.919
So having seen this and
having been in these workshops

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:47.380
all week, you might
immediately wonder,

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:51.110
is this just first
order model accounting?

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:54.770
Like, I could
write this sentence

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.440
so this part is
just propositional,

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.460
and there's one
relation here, f.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:08.270
And if I look at this formula
with this existential counting

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.700
quantifier--

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:15.560
yeah, so models
for this relation f

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:19.530
correspond to a Skolem function.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:22.830
And this is a first-order
sentence with two variables.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.460
It's in that fragment
that we can solve.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:29.840
What's the problem?

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.920
And the problem is tractable
for a first-order model

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.020
counting means polynomial
in the domain size.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:39.100
But in these cases,
we have m bits,

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:43.000
and so we end up with some sort
of a relatively large domain.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.200
So it's not exactly
tractable, so we need

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.580
we do need other techniques.

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:53.330
But the problem is
still a little bit hard,

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:56.150
so we changed things
a little bit more.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:10:02.380
It's immediate that exact
counting is #P-hard.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.170
Propositional model
counting is a special case.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:07.900
And so there's two
answers to this.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:10.150
One is we're going to
consider, of course,

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:13.180
randomized approximate counting.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:18.760
But moreover, we're also
going to need to use oracles

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:22.270
for these subroutines
of model counting

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:23.560
and approximate sampling.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:27.580
We're not going to use too
many model counting queries.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:31.332
Ultimately, it will
be two, but the number

00:10:31.332 --> 00:10:33.290
of these queries that we
use is, in some sense,

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:36.850
going to be the main complexity
measure for this thing.

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.370
Now, the other problem
here is the number

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:43.660
of possible functions is
potentially very large,

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:46.240
and I can't even
write down a number

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:51.950
that's this large in some
reasonable amount of time.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:56.480
And yeah, so the answer
here is, well, we're

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:58.850
going to just work with the
log of the number of Skolem

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.200
functions.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.450
And in cases where
maybe I was interested

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.403
in the entropy of that
distribution, then

00:11:04.403 --> 00:11:05.945
this is basically
what I want anyway.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.540
It's still, again, not a problem
that is immediately obvious how

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:17.310
to tackle in a scalable way.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.260
So the main results
that we have are

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:26.570
that we get an approx--
multiplicative approximate

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:30.600
count of the log of the
number of Skolem functions

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:36.420
given some polynomial number of
calls to approximate sampling

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:42.460
and counting oracles, and two
calls to an exact #SAT oracle.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:46.060
And in these
experiments, we will

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:49.930
see that it scales much better
than a totally naive baseline.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:55.580
Firstly, I hope that this is not
the last word on the problem,

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:59.300
and we'll come back
to some, hopefully,

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:02.950
potential ways it can
be improved later.

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:06.640
But our method is based
on the following three

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:08.740
key observations.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:13.510
The first is that the count
factorizes with respect

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.460
to the input.

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:25.010
I can set the value of f for
the different w's independently.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:29.650
And so in particular, when
I look at the log count,

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:36.240
this is just a big sum of the
log counts for each possible

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:38.310
binding of those
universal variables w's.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:45.960
Now second, we can partition the
domain into the following three

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:55.380
pieces, ones where there was no
response for the x variables,

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:59.040
ones where there's a unique
x that satisfies the formula,

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:02.390
and ones where
there's at least two.

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:09.500
And those first two pieces
have really specific counts

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.620
for the number of x's.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.230
Here, any x binding will work.

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:16.760
Here, there's a unique one.

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:18.710
And when I look at the log--

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:22.330
so yeah, that's
just m and that's 0.

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:25.840
And for this last
piece, what's nice

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:30.690
is that after I look
at the log, the number

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:38.900
of possible responses lies
somewhere in this range 1 to m,

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:42.920
and that's going to ensure that
I can approximate it easily.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.900
So with this framework
now, the algorithm

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:52.630
is actually pretty
straightforward.

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:58.740
So first of all, we're just
going to directly count

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:03.225
those inputs that don't have
a response x, and that's

00:14:03.225 --> 00:14:05.100
just going to be with
[INAUDIBLE] SAT oracle.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:06.165
That's one of our calls.

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:11.490
Now, the body of it
is actually going

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:17.790
to be estimating this
contribution from those w's

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:21.690
that had more than two, or
at least two, responses.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:25.260
So first I'm going to count
the number of w's that

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.420
had at least two responses.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.150
That's my second call.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:33.735
And I'm going to
invoke it on just

00:14:33.735 --> 00:14:34.860
counting with respect to w.

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:37.710
So this is a projected
model count for this formula

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:42.930
that captures that, yeah, there
were two distinct x responses.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.480
So obviously, this
is satisfiable

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:50.250
if, and only if, you're
in this set for w.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:56.700
And so then what I do now is--

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:01.950
now we're going to be
sampling universal variables

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:04.020
approximately
uniformly at random

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.000
by invoking our
approximate sampling

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:11.060
oracle on this formula.

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:14.450
And then we're going to use
the approximate counting

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:20.120
oracle for that w then to
approximate the count once,

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:25.350
I've fixed w in here, of the
number of possible x responses.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.290
And I'm just going to take
a running count of that

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:30.630
rescaled appropriately
from the number of samples

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:38.820
that I take ultimately up to the
actual number of these things

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:40.560
that have at least
two solutions.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.450
And so then yeah,
my final estimate

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:46.170
is just the pieces that had a
unique solution contribute 0

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.360
to the log count, so
this contributes m.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:51.940
And then this is my thing
that I got by sampling.

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:55.800
And in order to get a 1
plus epsilon multiplicative

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:01.428
approximation, because this log
count was in this range 1 to m,

00:16:01.428 --> 00:16:03.345
I only need this polynomial
number of samples.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.210
So it's not that
complicated, I hope.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.015
Let me-- are there any
questions about why this works?

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:18.991
No?

00:16:18.991 --> 00:16:22.150
AUDIENCE: In fact, you
only need a linear number

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:24.145
of proximal oracle [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:24.145 --> 00:16:25.270
BRENDAN JUBA: That's right.

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:37.140
So anyway, that's
about all I have

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:40.040
to say about this
theoretically right now.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:44.190
So onward-- implementation
experiments.

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:47.540
So there's an
important optimization.

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.900
Rather than just taking
a fixed size sample

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:55.070
that you would get from,
say, the Chernoff bound,

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:57.290
there was an algorithm
from Dagum et al

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:02.310
that adaptively chooses
a number of samples.

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.819
And it's giving us
the same guarantee

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:07.369
as if we had used a Chernoff
bound with a fixed size sample,

00:17:07.369 --> 00:17:11.780
but you're using fewer runs of
that approximate model counting

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:14.390
oracle sampling
and model counting,

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:17.359
and of course, that's really
important in practice.

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:18.329
So that's not our work.

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:22.130
So anyway, just we use it.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:26.420
And then we use, again, some
recent state of the art systems

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:30.140
for the various components.

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:35.340
And now, the naive baseline that
we're going to compare against.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:39.220
Like, really, this is the first
work to study this problem,

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:41.770
so we didn't really
have a good baseline.

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:47.100
So it kind of does
the naive thing.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:49.530
Everything is just
exact counting.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:53.700
I'm just enumerating
those w's that have

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.850
two distinct solutions, and I
do the exact count for them.

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:03.560
And obviously, this blows up.

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:10.560
So I guess it's a strawman, but
then again, what else is there?

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.020
And so, yeah, there's some
state of the art tools

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.860
for the enumeration count.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:23.280
So we have two types of
benchmarks that we look at.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:26.610
One is to examine the
efficiency and scaling,

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:30.720
and these are basically
benchmarks from works

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:32.890
on Skolem synthesis.

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:35.550
So it's these
[? QBF ?] benchmarks.

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:39.930
Now, it's going to turn out
that the baseline that we

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:43.730
have for getting those
exact counts really sucks.

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:47.550
So we also want
to do experiments

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:49.660
to check the
accuracy in practice,

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:53.250
so we need some really
easy benchmarks.

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:57.540
And so we use this 158 instances
that in particular have

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:59.520
very small input
sizes, and so where

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:00.810
the exact count can succeed.

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:10.660
So yeah, I mean, the story
for scaling is fairly simple.

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:13.630
Out of the 608 instances,
the naive baseline solves 8.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:17.170
There's the plot of
the running time.

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:22.030
And this new method
solves 379 out of the 608.

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:23.770
So obviously, it's
not everything,

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:27.640
but there's a big
improvement from actually

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:31.180
just literally enumerating all
of the possible w's and doing

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:33.880
this [INAUDIBLE] count.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:39.562
And if I look at, well,
how long would it take--

00:19:39.562 --> 00:19:41.020
look at the number
of counter calls

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:46.510
that would have been invoked
to solve these instances--

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:51.200
yeah, it's a very large
number of exact count calls.

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:56.780
So it's somewhat
implausible that you

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:59.090
would be able to do
this without doing

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:05.540
some kind of a sampling style
reduction in the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:11.300
So for accuracy, just
to stress the point,

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.840
the exact thing could only
solve 8 of those 608 instances,

00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:20.350
and that's not enough to get a
good measure of the accuracy,

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:23.640
so we use the other 158.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:28.140
And we're going to run
this thing with our epsilon

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.010
parameter being 0.8.

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:32.460
So the guarantee just
says that the count

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:39.990
should lie between 1/5 and
1.8 times the true log count.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:43.200
So that's what this upper
and lower bound is--

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:45.870
what's 0.8, what's 1/5.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:50.880
And so the actual
counts look like this.

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:56.290
And so some statistics on
the relative error here.

00:20:56.290 --> 00:21:01.450
So the mean relative
error was about a fifth,

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:07.810
so that's significantly better
than a relative error of 0.8.

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:11.980
And out of the 158 benchmarks,
the largest relative error

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:15.040
was less than a half.

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:18.880
So it does seem like it's
substantially outperforming

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:20.380
the guarantee that we have.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:26.450
Questions about this?

00:21:29.312 --> 00:21:30.770
AUDIENCE: You seem
not happy that I

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:31.700
would want to ask you, but--

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:33.290
BRENDAN JUBA: No,
I'm very happy.

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:34.915
I thought that
everybody was just going

00:21:34.915 --> 00:21:36.253
to be hibernating after lunch.

00:21:36.253 --> 00:21:38.420
AUDIENCE: So given that we
all [INAUDIBLE] circuits,

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:40.970
if the thing under
your quantifiers

00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:42.800
is something that
[INAUDIBLE] do you

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:44.660
have [INAUDIBLE] for
this Skolem count?

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:54.788
If every oracle call
is linear time--

00:21:54.788 --> 00:21:55.580
BRENDAN JUBA: Yeah.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:56.420
So I guess--

00:21:56.420 --> 00:22:01.730
I mean, the real question--
well, let me come back to this

00:22:01.730 --> 00:22:07.370
in like 30 seconds, because
this is my first open question.

00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:08.443
It's related to it--

00:22:08.443 --> 00:22:09.860
[? made ?] my first
and my second.

00:22:12.485 --> 00:22:14.860
So we introduced this new
problem of approximate counting

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:16.330
of Skolem functions.

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:18.640
And we proposed a
randomized algorithm

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.680
for a guaranteed
multiplicative approximation

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.300
to the log number of functions.

00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:24.670
We use the oracle for
exact counting twice,

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:28.030
and this linear number of calls
to the approximate sampling

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:31.150
and counting oracle.

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:35.530
We managed to get 379 out
of 608 of these benchmarks,

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:39.550
and we did outperform the
theoretical relative error

00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:42.160
guarantee on these
158 easy instances.

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:44.920
I don't know how good we're
doing on the 608 instances

00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:48.520
because we can't
solve them exactly.

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:51.520
But it seems plausible
that this method

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:54.220
or maybe some future
method along this line

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:58.090
could be useful in practice
for these problems.

00:22:58.090 --> 00:23:05.390
And again, just to stress,
counting models seemed hard,

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:06.910
but people worked on it.

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:09.730
Synthesizing Skolem
functions seemed hard--

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:11.800
people worked at it.

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:14.950
Counting Skolem functions
seems outrageous,

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:18.130
because I can't even write
down most Skolem functions.

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:20.660
They're just too big.

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:23.500
But nevertheless,
we are actually

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:27.820
able to get some what seem to be
plausibly useful approximations

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:30.160
to that count.

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:34.095
And so I think, really,
nobody thought about-- nobody

00:23:34.095 --> 00:23:35.470
dared to consider
what if I could

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:36.553
solve this problem before.

00:23:36.553 --> 00:23:39.230
And that's the reason for the
[HUMMING STAR WARS THEME SONG]..

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:44.910
So question-- so first of
all, is that exact model

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:47.980
counting necessary?

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:50.580
I used it twice.

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:55.140
I don't think I really need
it for the second time,

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:57.630
but for the first
one, what I actually

00:23:57.630 --> 00:24:04.020
wanted a good guarantee
for is the number

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:12.615
of universal bindings for which
there's no existential binding.

00:24:17.364 --> 00:24:21.600
We-- the naive way
to do this-- you

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.490
would like to just
negate that formula.

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:31.980
But if you started out saying,
well, I have-- whatever--

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:34.050
DNF, and so I have
my FPRAS for DNF,

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:36.420
let's say, and then
I'm negating that,

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:42.660
then I don't know that I'm
getting my same nice relative

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:45.270
multiplicative error guarantee.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:49.950
Maybe if-- so you have
another representation here,

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:52.240
your [? DDNF ?] or whatever.

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:55.800
And if you can get the
FPRAS for the count,

00:24:55.800 --> 00:25:03.300
and you can say push through
that negation, then hopefully,

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:06.810
you can just get rid of the need
for the exact model counting

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:07.950
and, indeed, get an FPRAS.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.530
So I hope that's true.

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:19.800
My question is really,
do I need it at all,

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:22.950
or how broadly do I need-- do
I actually need the exact model

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:25.950
counting oracle, because
that seems to be the main,

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:30.510
at least to me, barrier
to getting the FPRAS.

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:40.440
The other thing, which
you might be enticed by,

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:44.370
is we have many calls to this
approximate sample and count

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:48.270
oracle on the same formula.

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:52.440
This just sort of screams for
some kind of either compilation

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:58.020
or reuse across the
different iterations.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:25:59.370
How can you exploit this?

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:10.330
Kuldeep had some
motivation for extending

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:12.310
to more general
problems of counting

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:15.010
uninterpreted functions in SMT.

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:17.185
I do not feel qualified to
talk about this further.

00:26:20.530 --> 00:26:24.150
And finally, yeah, I
mean, I am interested

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:25.680
in other applications.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:28.590
So we're daring to ask
the question, what if we

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:30.210
could count Skolem functions?

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:32.666
Like, what can you do?

00:26:32.666 --> 00:26:35.860
It's like, please put on
your science fiction hat.

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:38.980
And yeah, your questions
for me go here.

00:26:48.115 --> 00:26:49.490
MODERATOR:
[INAUDIBLE] questions?

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:51.740
BRENDAN JUBA: We have
ample time for questions.

00:26:59.702 --> 00:27:02.160
MODERATOR: Does the count tell
